<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Semmering Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Semmering Railway is a mountain railway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> crossing the Semmering Pass between Gloggnitz and Mürzzuschlag, built between 1848 and 1854 to designs by Carl von Ghega. With its tunnels, viaducts and steep curves it was the first true mountain railway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and part of the line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/semmering-railway.jpg" alt="Semmering Railway" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C.Stadler/Bwag · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Semmering_-_Semmeringbahn,_20-Schilling-Blick.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Semmering Railway, which starts at Gloggnitz and leads over the Semmering to Mürzzuschlag, was the first mountain railway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> built with standard gauge track. It was constructed between 1848 and 1854 by some 20,000 workers under the project's designer and director, Carl von Ghega. The works include 14 tunnels, among them the 1,431 m summit tunnel, 16 viaducts, several of them two-storey, and over 100 stone arch bridges and 11 small iron bridges.</p><p>Over a track length of 41 km the line overcomes an altitude difference of 460 m. On 60% of its length the gradient is 2.0–2.5%, equivalent to a rise of one metre in 40, and 16% has a curve radius of only 190 m. This was an entirely new technical dimension of railway construction, and new instruments and methods of surveying had to be developed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A competition in 1851, the Semmering trials, decided which locomotives to buy. One stretch had gradients of 1 in 40 and curves with a minimum radius of 190 metres, and entrants had to hold a speed of 11.5 kilometres per hour with a maximum axle loading of 14 tonnes. Four entrants met the conditions but did not prove reliable in practice.</p><p>The railway itself used the Engerth design, in which the tender is articulated with the main locomotive frame so that part of the fuel and water weight rests on the driving wheels to improve adhesion, while the locomotive can still take relatively sharp curves. Sixteen Engerth locomotives were supplied between November 1853 and May 1854, and could climb a 1 in 40 gradient at 19 kilometres per hour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Even while it was being built, the railway was seen as a form of landscape gardening, an attempt to combine technology and nature harmoniously. The travel experience it offered contributed greatly to opening the Semmering region to tourism, and numerous hotels and mansions date from that epoch. The line remains part of the Southern Railway, operated by the Austrian Federal Railways. In 1998 it was added to the UNESCO World Heritage list.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Semmering Railway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Emperor Ferdinand Northern Railway began running in 1837, the <b>Semmering Railway</b> across the Alps was completed in 1854, and the Southern Railway linked Vienna with Trieste, the empire's main port and home of the Austrian Lloyd shipping company.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
